I can see the issue now. Let me look at the full context of both files. The issue seems to be related to margin handling for headings in Pendo. Let me check:

1. In `card.css` lines 139-144, there's a selector that adds margin to direct children that aren't `._pendo-row`:
```css
._pendo-step-container-styles > :is(div, form, textarea, h2):not(._pendo-row) {
  margin: var(--pendo-space-0) auto var(--instui-component-shared-tokens-spacing-general-space-sm) auto;
  width: 100%;
  max-width: var(--pendo-embedded-width);
}
```

This adds margin-bottom via the shorthand `margin`. 

2. In `text.css` (lines 139-147), there's a more specific selector for surveys:
```css
h2._pendo-simple-text,
._pendo-text-title ._pendo-simple-text,
h3._pendo-simple-text,
._pendo-text-subTitle ._pendo-simple-text,
._pendo-simple-text._pendo-nps-open-text-poll-question {
  font-weight: var(--instui-component-heading-title-card-mini-font-weight);
  font-family: var(--instui-component-heading-title-card-mini-font-family);
  font-size: var(--instui-component-heading-title-card-mini-font-size);
  line-height: var(--instui-component-heading-title-card-mini-line-height);
  margin: var(--pendo-space-0) var(--pendo-space-0)
    var(--instui-component-shared-tokens-spacing-general-space-sm);
}
```
This also has a margin-bottom defined. Comparing specificity, `card.css`'s `:is(div, form, textarea, h2):not(._pendo-row)` selector combined with the parent class comes to something like 0,1,1, while `text.css`'s `h2._pendo-simple-text` combines an element and a class for 0,1,1 as well — so it likely comes down to source order or additional context needed to fully resolve which wins.

one class, one element)

So the card.css rule should take precedence based on specificity. But there may be another factor—perhaps the heading is nested inside another wrapper element. I should check test files to see the actual DOM structure.

